5.1 Team Management
-Create team
-Add/remove members
-Assign roles (Admin/Editor/Viewer)
-Set parent team

5.2 Resource Access Logic
-Grant access to owner
-Grant access to owner’s team
-Grant access to parent teams (if hierarchy enabled)
-Apply collaborative overrides
-Apply sensitivity restrictions
-Apply explicit sharing rules

5.3 Team-Based Sharing
-Share resource with specific teams
-Choose share role: Viewer/Editor

5.4 Sensitive Resources
-Restrict default team visibility
-Allow only direct shares
-Indicators in UI (e.g., lock icon)

5.5 Section Assignment
-Assign section to team
-Auto-assign all team users
-Auto-carry permissions on reassign/move
		[KNOWN–UNKNOWN MATRIX (Section Assignment Feature)

I’m breaking it into four buckets:

Known Knowns → clear, decided behavior
Known Unknowns → we know the problem exists, but need decisions
Unknown Knowns → behavior that exists elsewhere (Loopio/RFPIO) that we must check
Unknown Unknowns → edge cases we must proactively surface

This is the strongest way to derisk the feature.

1. KNOWN KNOWNS (Clear rules you’ve already decided)

These are fixed truths based on your current model.

1.1 Section can be assigned to either:

A user

A team

1.2 If assigned to a team:

All team members get Edit permission over the section

Access updates dynamically when team membership changes

1.3 Section permission follows resource rules:

Owner always has full access

Collaborators always override team rules

Sensitive section overrides team access

If owner moves teams, team-based access recalculates

1.4 On reassigning section to another team:

Old team members lose edit access

New team members gain edit access

Collaborators remain the same

1.5 Nested hierarchy (if enabled):

Parent teams may see child-team sections (View)

Sensitive section = no hierarchy visibility

2. KNOWN UNKNOWNS (We know these decisions are pending)

These must be resolved before dev starts.

2.1 What is the default permission when a team is assigned?

Options:

Editor for all team members (current assumption)

Editor for team editors, Viewer for team viewers

Always editor, role ignored

Needs final decision.

2.2 What happens if section owner is in a different team than section-assigned team?

Example:

Section owned by User A (Team X)

Section assigned to Team Y

Should:

Team Y get edit

Team X only get view?

Or both get edit?

This must be explicitly defined.

2.3 Does section assignment persist if owner changes teams?

Owner moves from Team X → Team Z

Should the section still stay assigned to Team Y?

Or return to Team Z?

Or remain independent of owner’s team?

Needs clarity.

2.4 What if a team is deleted?

Behavior options:

Remove assignments

Move assignment to parent team

Mark section unassigned
Decision required.

2.5 How do you treat sub-teams in assignment?

Assign section to “Sales – APAC”.
Should parent “Sales” also get access?

Needs explicit definition.

2.6 What happens if a user has multiple teams?

Should the strongest permission across all teams apply?

Or only the assigned team matters?

Needs rule.

3. UNKNOWN KNOWNS (Other RFP tools already solved this, we must check)

These behaviors exist in Loopio / RFPIO that you may or may not want.

3.1 Reassignment notifications

Should Team Y members receive:

“You have been assigned Section 3”?
Should Team X receive:

“You no longer have access”?

Competitors do this.
Do you want it?

3.2 Section-level audit logs

Competitors track:

When section was assigned

To which team

By whom

When reassigned

Do you want this?

3.3 Deadline behavior

If a section has a due date:

Should the date apply to the team?

Should assignment change due dates?

RFPIO uses team-level deadlines.

Needs decision.

3.4 Multi-team assignment

Loopio allows ONE assignment per section.
RFPIO sometimes allows multiple reviewers.

Do you want:

One team per section

OR many teams per section?

Critical to define now.

3.5 Team role filtering in UI

Some tools hide teams with only “Viewer” role from assignment dropdown.

Do you want this?

4. UNKNOWN UNKNOWNS (Potential edge cases that can break your model)

These must be proactively identified.

4.1 What if section is marked sensitive AFTER assignment?

Should sensitive override team assignment?
(Current model says yes)
We need to define UI/notification behavior.

4.2 What if owner gets removed from org but section is assigned to a team?

Who becomes owner?
Team admin?
Project owner?
First team member?

Must be defined.

4.3 Team membership change mid-progress

User B joins Team Y → gets access
User B leaves Team Y → loses access

Need to confirm:

Should existing comments/answers remain visible?

Should they become hidden?
Loopio hides everything.

4.4 Assignment of empty sections

If a section is empty:

Should assignment do nothing?

Or should assignment mean “this team owns all upcoming work”?

Needs clarity.

4.5 Project-level access conflicting with section assignment

Example:
User can access Section 3 (assigned to team),
but cannot access Project (no project-level access).

Do we allow:

Section-only access?

Or require project access to see section?

Must be defined to avoid broken UX.

4.6 Team with no members

Assigning a section to an empty team.
Should:

System block it?

Allow and warn?

Auto-assign team admin only?

Decision needed.

4.7 Conflicts between explicit sharing and team assignment

If a user is:

Removed from team

But remains in explicit share list
Should access drop to view?
Or remain editor?

Must define precedence.

